# The source for how this project is cited. GitHub reads it to render the
# "Cite this repository" button; `luria index` derives the README's BibTeX
# block from it, so the two cannot disagree.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use Luria, please cite it as below.
title: "Luria: project memory, kept honest by lint"
abstract: >-
  A record of what a project knows and of what it no longer believes:
  referable documents with citable identities and standing, generated views,
  and a lint that reports every citation of something that has gone out of
  force.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Marx
    given-names: David
repository-code: https://github.com/dmarx/luria
url: https://dmarx.github.io/luria/
license: MIT
keywords:
  - project-memory
  - architecture-decision-records
  - documentation
  - truth-maintenance
# No `version:` or `date-released:`. The version is derived from the release
# tag (ADR-053), and repeating it here would be the hand-maintained copy that
# decision removed — stale on the next release, in the one file people paste
# into bibliographies. Add the release you used to the entry you paste.
